 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "WESTBURY WHITE HORSE. Look right! A horse 180 feet long is cut into the chalk hill. An older horse is said to have marked King Alfred's victory at Edington; the present one dates from 1778 and is much more elegant."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "SALISBURY PLAIN. 300 square miles of chalk downland, much of it used by the army. Red flags mean 'no walking'. White flags mean 'perhaps walking'. Pale blue flags mean 'we have given up and gone for tea'."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "BARROWS. Bronze Age burial mounds, 4,000 years old, dotted across the downs. Please do not dig. The Archaeological Society will be along shortly with trowels and very strong opinions."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 57,
   "text": "STONEHENGE: 5 MILES. Follow the track. If you hear a deep, distant humming, that is the stones, or a tractor, or the army. Probably the army."
  }
 ],
